---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import LinkArrow from '../components/LinkArrow.astro';
import { dictionaryModes } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Dictionary | Eloqium TTS"
  description="Learn how Eloqium TTS user dictionaries control text replacements and pronunciations, matching modes, language scope, JSON files, and compatible IBM .dic imports."
  currentPath="/dictionary/"
>
  <div class="shell"><PageIntro eyebrow="Dictionary" title="Customize the way individual words and phrases are spoken." description="The User Dictionary is useful when a name, abbreviation, technical term, or other piece of text needs a different reading from the one produced by the normal speech-processing path. You can replace the incoming text or provide a pronunciation directly." /></div>

  <section class="page-section" aria-labelledby="entry-types-heading">
    <div class="shell"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Entry types</p><h2 id="entry-types-heading">Choose between text replacement and pronunciation.</h2><p class="section-summary">Both types of entry are stored in the same dictionary system, but they serve different purposes.</p></div>
      <div class="content-grid two-up">
        <article class="content-card feature-card-accent"><span class="card-index">01</span><h3>Text replacement</h3><p>A text entry changes the source phrase into another piece of text before it is synthesized. This is useful when you want an abbreviation expanded, a name altered, or a particular spelling replaced with a form that produces the reading you expect. For example, an entry can replace <code>BRB</code> with <code>be right back</code>.</p></article>
        <article class="content-card feature-card-accent"><span class="card-index">02</span><h3>Pronunciation</h3><p>A pronunciation entry is useful when changing the spelling is not enough. It stores an OpenEVV Standard Phonemic Representation pronunciation so that a particular source term can be synthesized using the pronunciation you specify.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section alt" aria-labelledby="matching-heading">
    <div class="shell split-section">
      <aside><p class="section-kicker">Matching</p><h2 id="matching-heading">Choose how broadly an entry should apply.</h2><div class="aside-note"><p>The safest starting point is usually Exact match. The other modes are useful when the same entry should apply to a wider range of text.</p></div></aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <dl class="definition-list dictionary-definitions">{dictionaryModes.map((mode) => <div><dt>{mode.name}</dt><dd>{mode.detail}</dd></div>)}</dl>
        <h3>Case sensitivity</h3>
        <p>Each entry has a case-sensitivity setting. A case-sensitive rule distinguishes uppercase and lowercase letters, while a case-insensitive rule can match without requiring the same letter case. When otherwise comparable rules overlap, the case-sensitive rule is given higher precedence.</p>
        <h3>Precedence</h3>
        <p>When several dictionary rules could apply to the same part of the input, Eloqium resolves them deterministically. Longer source text is considered before shorter source text; within the same source length, exact matching is preferred to broader matching modes; case-sensitive rules are preferred over case-insensitive rules when the preceding criteria are equal; and the original dictionary order provides the final tie-breaker.</p>
        <p>Rules are applied as a single pass over the original input. A replacement produced by one dictionary entry is not fed back through the dictionary system to trigger another replacement.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section" aria-labelledby="scope-heading">
    <div class="shell capability-layout">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Language scope</p><h2 id="scope-heading">Keep dictionaries associated with the language they are meant for.</h2><p>Dictionaries belong to a regional language such as <code>en-US</code> or <code>fr-CA</code>. When Eloqium is synthesizing speech for one regional language, entries assigned to another regional language do not become active for that request. You can maintain multiple named dictionaries for a language and enable or disable each dictionary independently.</p></div>
      <div class="callout"><p class="callout-label">Useful examples</p><ul><li>A person’s name that is pronounced unexpectedly.</li><li>An acronym that should be expanded or spoken differently.</li><li>A specialist term that needs a consistent pronunciation.</li><li>A regional word or phrase that should be read in a particular way.</li></ul></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section alt" aria-labelledby="backup-heading">
    <div class="shell split-section"><aside><p class="section-kicker">Import and export</p><h2 id="backup-heading">Keep your dictionaries portable.</h2></aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h3>JSON dictionaries</h3>
        <p>Eloqium uses a versioned JSON format for dictionary storage and exchange. The current schema is version 2. Version 1 dictionary files are accepted by the parser and can be imported into the current system.</p>
        <p>A dictionary file can contain its language, name, enabled state, entries, and optional provenance information. Entries retain their replacement text, matching mode, case-sensitivity choice, and entry type.</p>
        <h3>IBM <code>.dic</code> files</h3>
        <p>Eloqium can import compatible legacy IBM <code>.dic</code> files as an external migration format. The importer distinguishes pronunciation entries from ordinary text replacements, records the original filename, and creates imported entries with Exact match and case-sensitive matching as their initial settings.</p>
        <p>The importer uses the filename as a source for its initial language and dictionary-layer proposal. It does not inspect the dictionary content to semantically determine a language or layer. Character decoding is attempted as UTF-8 first, followed by Windows-1252 when invalid UTF-8 replacement characters are detected; an ISO-8859-1 fallback is used when the decoding attempt itself fails.</p>
        <p>When the inferred source language and selected target language are not considered compatible, the importer reports a warning rather than silently changing the target language. Android file managers and compatible sharing applications can pass supported JSON and <code>.dic</code> files to Eloqium’s Dictionary Importer.</p>
        <p>Open <strong>Settings → User Dictionary → Dictionaries</strong> in the application to create and manage dictionaries.</p>
        <LinkArrow href="/guide/" label="Read the dictionary setup steps" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
